findings.body:
**A killed fix round's uncommitted edits reach the next round's worker**

`pup kill` on a session in a fix round now goes back to `merged` instead of `killed`. It stops the tmux window, but the worktree that `restoreWorktree` brought back stays registered on `pup/<id>`, along with anything the dead worker left uncommitted (edited or untracked files):

```ts
if (!isTerminal(row.state) && newest?.type === 'fix_round') {
  transitionSessionAlong(db, sessionId, { from: row.state, to: FIX_ROUND_EDGE.from }, { kind: 'fix-round-killed' });
  return FIX_ROUND_EDGE.from;
}
```
[session-lifecycle.service.ts#L559-L578](https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/pupitre/blob/b71a5a75ed59f914fed4f11e085d1ce6358b168a/src/core/session-lifecycle.service.ts#L559-L578)

If the worker committed nothing, the branch tip is still the PR head, and `pup fix` accepts the session again: it only requires `state === 'merged'` plus the merge, pin and review checks, none of which look at the kill. `restoreWorktree` then takes the surviving-worktree branch, which checks only HEAD and branch and never runs `git status`:

```ts
if (registered) {
  if (!registered.includes(`HEAD ${head}`) || !registered.includes(`branch ${branchRef}`)) { ...throw }
  return false;
}
```
[fix-round.service.ts#L356-L365](https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/pupitre/blob/b71a5a75ed59f914fed4f11e085d1ce6358b168a/src/core/fix-round.service.ts#L356-L365)

So the new round's worker opens in a dirty tree that holds the killed round's half-made edits. Its brief frames that tree as the PR head the review read, and tells it to "Commit the fixes on this branch". Three things contradict this:
- Decision 90 says a kill means the round's work is abandoned.
- `restoreWorktree`'s own JSDoc says a surviving worktree that "may hold work the gate never measured" is refused, not reused.
- The kill message in `src/cli/index.ts` warns only about the round's *commits*.

Before this delta the path could not happen: a killed round ended as `killed`, and `pup fix` refuses that state. The gate's `worktree-clean` stage limits the damage, since nothing ungated lands, but abandoned work is easily committed into the next round. The kill test in fix-round.test.ts never runs `pup fix` again after the kill.

Possible fixes:
- Refuse a registered worktree that has local changes: a `status --porcelain` check inside the `if (registered)` branch, after `assertNoArmedGitDrivers`, since status can run filters.
- Or have the kill path remove the worktree, as `pup land` does.
